#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548275} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee86905-f590-503c-8720-85bf192e1221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(- 6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548277} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5c3ddf-a226-5fb1-8ac1-23c3bb6fc29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x - 2}\\left(- 6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548292} {:answer/id #uuid "53fef7c3-564b-508d-bbe5-96ce9d4af498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x - 2}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548297} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5aff36-c237-5c29-96f2-1540d685c947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(3 x^{2} + 6 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548275} :answer-field/id #uuid "279f0fe1-f8e4-5afa-b477-f2e27ef72c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $x - 2\\leq y\\leq 0$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=4 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle|_{z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(3 y\\right)\\left(2 x\\right)-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(4 y\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 6 x y)+(12 x y)+(3)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/667d3cc98a0951d2c6fa4be3684d0ac37c6c7307d2f38f56c9444593492e25f5.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0d836e-54a3-5340-9b13-dcc36232e947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548276} {:answer/id #uuid "2058e903-53c1-5c81-9185-9c31af4d09f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548284} {:answer/id #uuid "34c4690f-7fa6-5adf-99a3-e7e66e0f4675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548290} {:answer/id #uuid "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548311} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa3c0f4-aada-5874-bf25-4f4d4627107b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1111b70b-db9b-5b6e-800f-d01f0ede213c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2 - x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle|_{z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16\\right)\\left(- 2 x\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 2 x \\left(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} + 16\\right))+(0)+(0)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef88b67d11b3848190625d9329f220d0c213cb8252bd1d0de22ba8de035678b.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbae015-9c86-576e-9542-d658ec05d8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548294} {:answer/id #uuid "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548313} {:answer/id #uuid "a5503768-564a-5162-9311-2202462dd105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548316} {:answer/id #uuid "b517f91d-49bb-51a2-bb5d-82c3c6c010ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548318} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e7d55b-c344-5ef3-a707-0a8e86cff164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548313} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19f56d4-a3af-5c69-a1b9-2d85f2264a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 6 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x + 18)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[18 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(18 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=18 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36fade4b-e6ad-561d-a3f5-aa58e1863d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 162 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548291} {:answer/id #uuid "5175042d-2d39-5599-87d4-e96b16f146f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 450 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548295} {:answer/id #uuid "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 612 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548308} {:answer/id #uuid "f64a0dba-2001-5ad1-8d0a-6f0446345425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 336 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548333} {:answer/id #uuid "fcabb8f4-cd17-54eb-81e2-ebbe693a7554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "612 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548308} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f987d97-8b0c-5075-a0a3-9441e76eccc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 x - 4\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle|_{z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} - 4 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 x - 4\\right)-\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 y + 4\\right)+\\left(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 x - 4\\right))+(\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 y + 4\\right))+(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[- 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) - 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) + 32 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(- 16 \\pi r^{3} + 16 r^{2} - 64 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(16 r^{2}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(16 \\pi r \\left(- r^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\pi r^{4} - 32 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(- 288 \\pi\\right)+\\left(- 324 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=- 612 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548327} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7db780-8908-5832-a157-32ca134dc1bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x - y + 3$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$ The circulation is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2}\\int_0^{2}\n$$ {{field-1}} $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x - y + 3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle|_{z=- x - y + 3}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x y\\right)\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(4 y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((4 x y)+(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)+(4 y)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1f/1f3f8869870631489863fa51c3b3297626962e265c43938fb1fa10273ec8e677.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064c81e9-cac4-5b54-aab3-071b92c05f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548271} {:answer/id #uuid "52609f73-07a8-520a-a473-dcfa11de11b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548296} {:answer/id #uuid "776a83db-0de1-5556-a705-63fc901967dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548304} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548314} {:answer/id #uuid "a13070d2-5fd5-5d85-a19f-8d85e967eafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548314} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb448220-651b-59de-aca5-26472ab8b179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=2}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 4)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(36 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=36 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006d6dfe-0844-5971-9c61-382cbb28d0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548270} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548273} {:answer/id #uuid "3028c6da-4535-5dec-ad67-803e3189ac38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "225 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548289} {:answer/id #uuid "5afa31f9-d938-51cb-b486-71ac3e40af68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548298} {:answer/id #uuid "90fcb849-8f04-5003-8a20-d247a6ce6d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548273} :answer-field/id #uuid "1194953f-36a6-55fd-86be-9f2bd4fd97cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6222ad0-cdab-5e3b-b25d-c7228bf6bc82" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=4$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(4\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(4\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=4}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 16\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 16)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[16 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(144 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=144 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b21800-0e88-5dbb-a9a1-66f5cccf128e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548272} {:answer/id #uuid "6b94038e-032c-5029-a77e-c204b7c5d549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548300} {:answer/id #uuid "804436a9-64c7-584d-bd22-73da3b39a9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548307} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f9c2ea-45a4-530b-a88e-c343c3142e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548322} {:answer/id #uuid "def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548328} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce7b7e5-6f3c-584c-bca6-6514b9a1b744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc0d6a0e-f5b6-5657-a4c8-70261c68ec76" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2 x.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x\\right)=2\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle|_{z=2 x}\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(0\\right)\\left(2\\right)-\\left(4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e90636-c836-54ff-81ae-058a934ee6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548288} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6cb252-46c3-558d-bcc6-d52eee217622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548309} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fe49c8-710f-5b92-9a08-bb94ad638ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548323} {:answer/id #uuid "d0eaa6cc-4368-55af-b243-68c7b79a2981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548325} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548330} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2f5e54-e210-5fdc-8326-8e2ab4e24cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e276ccb8-03fe-518f-a12f-012c8be3b237" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=1.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=1}\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 1)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[\\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(9 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=9 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c08049-201c-5042-a365-7c340c66df2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549262} {:answer/id #uuid "683912d5-dc99-556c-8497-b75eb1cc6fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549269} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ca081f-4cd9-5b7f-aa44-2df4fd0e0372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549276} {:answer/id #uuid "a50b2500-22cc-56bc-96fb-e31907f23529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549278} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db3bbf-4025-5d56-b788-262363990733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549276} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf60312b-a12d-5e96-b6e5-ac8aa867616f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13892e3f-d765-51b3-b960-59a3b31629f7" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 8 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-8 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598862ab-da77-5c3e-950f-424d0dbeafd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4 \\sqrt{u v w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549267} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6c4b9e-e984-59b1-a0f4-ec0de03ed2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4 \\sqrt{u v w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549268} {:answer/id #uuid "98b94f67-6fda-5dd5-838b-c871de8b8565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 \\sqrt{u v w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549275} {:answer/id #uuid "cec7cae4-a5b4-56d3-abbb-799c79365d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8 \\sqrt{u v w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549288} {:answer/id #uuid "d995e84d-143b-5deb-8ce1-9f47274df5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{u v w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549267} :answer-field/id #uuid "744b238d-934b-5bda-a379-baac5d22b3f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "28f499ce-a5b7-5f3f-90af-72bf76f6b98e" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2\\left(xy\\right) \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=2\\left(xy\\right),\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2\\left(y\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2\\left(x\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScaling the three derivative rows by $2, 1, 1$ multiplies the determinant by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(y\\right) & 2\\left(x\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\left(y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\  & =2\\left(y(xz-0)-x(0-yz)\\right)\\\\  & =2\\left(xyz+xyz\\right)\\\\  & =4 x y z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(2xyz\\right) & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)vw}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4 \\sqrt{u v w}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062576af-9242-58c0-acd7-1495f4c576a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549250} {:answer/id #uuid "5081b46a-4548-5033-9007-286761c7841f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549265} {:answer/id #uuid "90358761-bee7-511c-98b3-d801c0ad1333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549273} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e0a6e3-94eb-5f33-b1b0-6e5fad482c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549280} {:answer/id #uuid "be40c444-ea57-54fc-9a2f-fc878b0ca2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549265} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c746ba5-b294-58eb-b858-b452b710eccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3fd142b6-9fe1-551e-8de2-205d9c2a38c6" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-2 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044ebbb0-49a4-5e07-aa5f-52071f46d7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549249} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9d74b8-e21a-5070-b867-4bb5e1c969bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549256} {:answer/id #uuid "2d993ae9-8ed2-5193-82c6-1154b2659b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549260} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcacf0f-5374-5915-8bc2-3f0e72e2b38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549271} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3f7b1e-e9e6-5da0-a816-27f38a49b857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549271} :answer-field/id #uuid "dabd80d4-e1e0-5326-9f3e-7bad3d5ea123" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "911ff483-5437-5b63-bdea-c53a9f75354d" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=6 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=6 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=6\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a667fa8-98a7-5e3a-8f33-7e8c395fff89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{u v w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549254} {:answer/id #uuid "a786dab9-5919-5759-956b-993feb670569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u v w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549279} {:answer/id #uuid "b638164a-9ad0-5829-ae5a-23171cfb7180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3 u v w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549281} {:answer/id #uuid "d6974a06-9662-5b61-acf6-ca24df7c0d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{u v w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549292} {:answer/id #uuid "f609c86c-9d04-561b-a7d4-2ad7fad72584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u v w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549296} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16b4cca-18ce-58fd-8bd2-69c764b59ccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a4d85b42-ead4-58c7-a443-af6077a7bc6a" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) \\\\ 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) \\\\ 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=3\\left({e}^{x}\\right),\\,v(x,y,z)=3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right),\\,w(x,y,z)=2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScaling the three derivative rows by $3, 3, 2$ multiplies the determinant by $18$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0)\\right)\\\\  & =18 e^{x} e^{x + y} e^{x + y + z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\left({e}^{3x+2y+z}\\right) & =18\\left({e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{w}{2}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u v w}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09578f3c-e56b-5d41-8c71-363f402b8a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549251} {:answer/id #uuid "2117d2fe-e22b-580e-b937-660440cfb351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549257} {:answer/id #uuid "54e5e4b1-e24e-5724-84de-b57e55cf0379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549266} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff7a6e2-6145-5517-8fe6-a62e7e2c4bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549272} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf36b41-43d1-55d2-8d9b-9b8cd572e3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549272} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae9d39c-099c-5bd8-a0ca-bc8e7b7b3159" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b767531b-eb1e-5afa-bf86-ac2bd324f05f" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 4 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-4 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-4\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-4\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c259f24-71e7-5c81-8c15-fdc786279f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549252} {:answer/id #uuid "492caa10-5fcf-5397-b994-6b200973c910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549264} {:answer/id #uuid "c48c888d-7743-558d-9d46-8fdf3b8894c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549287} {:answer/id #uuid "d6308f6d-4622-5816-aab9-b671c0c55be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549291} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b64f2c-68d2-58ae-a2c1-0d38b84e4e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549287} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb6301b-47d8-5a7f-8598-fe21b9f7e95c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceca63e6-2d57-5912-bbab-1955792822f8" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 5 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-5 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-5\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-5\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67d2eb68-0652-5e48-b001-a45c2700c7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549515} {:answer/id #uuid "94798f5c-574c-53cd-bb0e-eb9396410fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549605} {:answer/id #uuid "c199e1eb-7983-554d-81aa-b675e5252dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549680} {:answer/id #uuid "e8394076-ef1d-5d29-b139-67414f969f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549743} {:answer/id #uuid "ee167035-8819-5d49-80a8-31c9bebffaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549515} :answer-field/id #uuid "63772144-9630-582a-8d79-f08320bc64ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e8e30b7-68c9-50ac-bfbf-42853c6a4c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161305" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,xz⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+bu{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-u)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =-2u{v}^{2}-u{v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-3u{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot {u}^{2}{v}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbe30bb-dd70-51c4-9f43-070d0318ff7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=7\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550218} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6c7d34-e449-5d73-af74-0a34c3cfaaee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta-4,\\,y=7\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550223} {:answer/id #uuid "526ab47d-4703-5f19-8c4f-b71ee67409a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=14\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550224} {:answer/id #uuid "80678170-c1ea-5027-b0fa-8187fe611477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=49\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=9\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550231} {:answer/id #uuid "d0311b93-9073-5580-9f0d-63a6fa706932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550218} :answer-field/id #uuid "9397b61d-948d-504f-950b-18860c8f3421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "20515a44-66d9-5ff7-84aa-7d04800bd82f" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(4,0)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $7.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,0),$ $a=3$ and $b=7.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=7\\sin\\theta,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324c4b93-5639-568a-a241-77105436aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=12\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550220} {:answer/id #uuid "cae46619-3058-5aa2-8e9f-5213242a465e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550237} {:answer/id #uuid "cf57cc26-9b3b-5042-8225-7de688c091a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550238} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c3d618-0f0e-5daa-a8c5-99b065d44834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta+1,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550241} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cb44cf-21ae-55a8-a15b-460069cc03c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=36\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=16\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550237} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8839b84-0f3f-5a19-a4fe-0ca9fa129882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2239f992-7633-5589-888e-9634e4ba5dbc" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(-1,2)$ with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $6.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=4$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta+2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fdafd4-dfd0-5942-8d1d-9ab45838aec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=25\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=9\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550213} {:answer/id #uuid "2a524aaf-b8dc-5c56-97c5-1231ac8b3a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550217} {:answer/id #uuid "b07e57bc-095b-5619-949d-f34eea939c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=10\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550235} {:answer/id #uuid "fb70ee01-36b4-54d8-9cfb-68a754f57f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550246} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb597f3-d95c-5f0a-acf9-d5539cbeec14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta-4,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550217} :answer-field/id #uuid "d663aa2d-8929-5530-8d94-a5f352121f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "63d70940-3d71-5690-b5be-4ed950a5e8df" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(4,-2)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256d8302-1d45-5644-bac4-241c84aa075a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=49\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550216} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae9ad02-bbc5-507e-9d39-69899081a3b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550221} {:answer/id #uuid "3f113afe-e8d8-5af8-97c1-a649307c55c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550222} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0184da-da30-5a4e-81f1-d784246a7dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=14\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550226} {:answer/id #uuid "7b356d09-d65c-58b0-ba25-e9c3e505ec7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=7\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550222} :answer-field/id #uuid "3067012f-88ab-59d3-9951-48a4baaf008a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90ee6bd1-96fc-55ab-8ca6-ab5f01d48cc5" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-6)$ with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $3.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-6),$ $a=7$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta-6,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66bf8e98-f6c5-5f55-8e80-a6d4075a8f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550228} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4e2830-d507-5868-b998-561389872ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550230} {:answer/id #uuid "8ced2143-4ac3-53f8-bee3-51fdd586b9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550232} {:answer/id #uuid "f359a5d8-2f51-5b9f-92eb-b8c86c9b0e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta-4,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550242} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b6fdb4-d469-502f-ac9f-2ba4f4469652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=9\\sin\\theta+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550230} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa406e7-a8b3-57bf-84f2-80a82309a300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f616e421-93b6-544a-b9b7-6dc0cbf6e25b" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(4,3)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $4.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,3),$ $a=3$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta+3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b6a044-942f-5f80-b14d-73ed378bca93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550262} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a2da4d-6210-5be9-81a0-ecf8b36f9fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550304} {:answer/id #uuid "d35e7323-a3a8-5279-b564-2450b3823623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550312} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ed603a-a6d1-59ec-a92d-3cb73aaa6f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550319} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0d6cf6-2603-558d-a00a-f5ea02b21f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550304} :answer-field/id #uuid "adeaaac1-383a-5cad-9711-2562527f01ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "004c532d-124d-5053-9b4b-e5607aeffc93" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=4 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{2}{5 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=5 & ⟹\\, & u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d3dd7067bbcb0a6e3c901d12a2795500e192594a14e7f1373dd325e4fd270363.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091b43cb-3824-5278-b02a-80954580abf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550253} {:answer/id #uuid "0a734b23-70c8-5a87-b181-df0896bb13b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550254} {:answer/id #uuid "178d842d-884a-503a-aed8-8f5a489cdee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550257} {:answer/id #uuid "4e095de2-8e72-5c9f-a025-ba50adefeaf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550273} {:answer/id #uuid "788699c0-c7c8-57d6-9abc-ef6f6c7c2cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550257} :answer-field/id #uuid "783e65fb-53de-5f75-a984-ad63c97517df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f546348-0394-5bd9-acc6-4dd4084bdee8" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=2 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15f5caab85f05d666bb383692bad6b67d04da853f1412505a62ba6aba7ce36d6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{8 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{8 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{8 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{6}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 6-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0904b7b1-cfa8-5ae6-aa60-5aca7ae33f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550252} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f8a2d-b59e-5889-a32b-9f1d2fac5c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + 7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550271} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6f989e-1b5b-5f7d-97a4-2ef1d6359863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(21 \\right)}}{2} + 3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550283} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc730c1-3ddc-5798-aa10-5faa26d2866e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550294} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bf27c2-7f80-5b22-9f0e-84d04ea44933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550294} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bcfa681-838b-590c-8d5c-00b8d4d36aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fce4d70-31bd-5ea0-bd52-007df134c134" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=3 x^{2},\\qquad y=7 x^{2},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{8}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\,v=x y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{x^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=7 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=7 & ⟹\\, & u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{8}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=8 & ⟹\\, & v=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 8\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd1c5b38643b6c5e0ceded7b3cae3d06e51135f84fb9598a129e8627b55c14a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 y}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3 y}=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3 u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{7}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{8}1\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 3)\\cdot (8-1) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34eda6cd-67a8-5208-9e75-fe5046749335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550266} {:answer/id #uuid "51907818-a2e8-522c-aa69-39eb4bf60dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550276} {:answer/id #uuid "58e3803f-728c-5865-b3dc-3ad15dd8df31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550280} {:answer/id #uuid "a0633187-d739-5c3d-8142-90bf6a8cd65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(15 \\right)}}{2} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550301} {:answer/id #uuid "dea6270f-e136-5864-8dee-bf3860074050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27887e3-37e9-5379-9335-951ca05cfc34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6524cd37-7830-594b-a844-cca2bae3152d" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\qquad v=3 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{3}{8 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=3 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 8,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/80/80a923224b455c6f8cfa17a7099c1bb86206a115845954af6123bfb6b3ef5138.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 9 x^{2} y & 3 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{18 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{18 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{18 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{8}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{8}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 5-\\ln 3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e7b4f0-779a-5592-82fe-d0c0df5c9448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550256} {:answer/id #uuid "29655adf-66c1-523f-b8f7-b67feb1b58bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550264} {:answer/id #uuid "57adb191-6307-50ae-87c3-b23763d25030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550278} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4e10bf-6c08-5075-aca9-e62df0216285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550293} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e41a24-8fa6-5600-b8a5-7d2b62d4d248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550314} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f147d4-75e8-5313-8ada-be150ea18f49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86313c0f-17d0-58ab-b4b7-aaf7ffda4b59" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=4 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x - 2,\\qquad y=2 x + 2,\\qquad y=2 - 4 x,\\qquad y=4 - 4 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=4 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/434447c0d9ece418e17a0ac3f0cc044faf76ba5689ed692fc3408cb78501326c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3838494d-4bc0-57ba-8752-ecc004226996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550268} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef72dfb-cff7-5042-8cb9-d26a99b2f68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550269} {:answer/id #uuid "5140b796-9850-5bf3-9ac2-45cc99ff75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550275} {:answer/id #uuid "c8aafbcd-c95b-5422-8726-47cc5dbfbe4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550307} {:answer/id #uuid "f096027c-d8bd-5a97-9ddf-45dab5ab5bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550268} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcf5418-3547-52bd-81b4-32f2ae8aea43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e3bead7-3041-5d6a-a3c9-e99ea7492182" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\qquad v=x^{2} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{8 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\,v=x^{2} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x^{2} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 2 x y & x^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=y=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{x^{2} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2386a1bb-f698-5731-ac56-eae73d9c2526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550261} {:answer/id #uuid "364f4a6e-3884-5e11-898e-195e0f488998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550267} {:answer/id #uuid "42dd8959-7c57-53cb-96d3-5073891634e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550270} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d24d64-b125-59c2-9c5e-b2e0878caeb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550303} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e81a80-bfe3-51d9-993d-f8507bc8d457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550317} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4d613b-bc83-54c0-a0e4-d701b6438ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9eb1a8f5-9688-520c-9d88-de87948baa7e" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x,\\qquad y=2 x + 2,\\qquad y=3 - x,\\qquad y=4 - x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=4 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,3\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2c3cb5e8b7c7448b5f90e372e20e6ff2bd0e547dd085d34ac3103178083ce8c2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{3}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012e1875-ab96-5a40-b850-8d7642320250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550251} {:answer/id #uuid "1f603556-ac3a-53ce-886e-ebae835e98e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550259} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbb1a99-4a86-56b6-a41b-dd3b2bed1c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550260} {:answer/id #uuid "d75c08a4-2365-566a-a3f8-bdf61e29faf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550316} {:answer/id #uuid "fef4672b-8b6b-5412-ad9f-f6bbf1aa9954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550251} :answer-field/id #uuid "58740935-52c9-58bc-be07-3f8d77109e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ccb83066-0219-5269-b997-9cdb213c5816" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=4 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
